    "question": {
      ":question/id": "c0c0ec96-2f3c-5671-81fe-ace29aeee342",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8x-1}}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=8x-1.$ Then $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}={u}^{-1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\cdot (8) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(8x-1{)}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "060a2393-09a8-5068-bc4b-8c6cc5675e0c",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
